  "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2{x}^{3}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ [2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 6{x}^{2}]+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{2}y+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+4y) & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-6{x}^{2}y}{2{x}^{3}+4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
